<del></del></span> Emperor Shaohao</span></h3>
<div class="pad exclude-units"></div>
<div class="pad exclude-units"></div>Emperor Shaohao is a new faction introduced on the Timeless Isle.  This faction is purely for vanity loot only.<h2 class="heading-size-2 heading-bar" id="hit-expertise">
<span data-markup-content-target="1">
<span class="iconmedium" data-color="c6" data-contiguous="true" data-env="mists" data-game="wow" data-kind="round" data-tree="mists">
<ins class="lazy-load-background" style='background-image: url("https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/medium/ability_marksmanship.jpg");'></ins>
<del></del></span> Reaching Hit and Expertise Cap for Unholy Death Knights</span></h2>In Mists of Pandaria, your Hit cap is 7.5% Hit or 2550 Hit Rating and 7.5% Expertise or 2550 Expertise Rating. Keep in mind that some abilities, such as the  <span class="icontiny lazy-load-background" data-contiguous="true" style='background-image: url("https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/race_draenei_female.gif");'></span> Draenei Racial Heroic Presence, will contribute to your overall Hit and/<wbr/>or Expertise needed.<h4 class="heading-size-4" id="hit-rating">Hit Rating</h4>At 7.5% Hit, your special (yellow damage) hits will no longer be able to miss raid bosses. This effectively means your abilities will never miss, though your regular swings still might. <br/>
<br/>It is even more important to be Hit capped as a Pet class like Unholy Death Knight, since both your Ghoul and Gargoyle inherit your Hit chance and will miss if not capped.<h4 class="heading-size-4" id="expertise-rating">Expertise Rating</h4>At 7.5% Expertise, raid bosses will no longer be able to dodge your attacks. They will, however, still be able to parry them. For the vast majority of raid bosses, you will be positioned behind them, and enemies cannot parry attacks from behind.<br/>
<br/>Expertise also grants Spell Hit in Mists of Pandaria, making it much more important than earlier. Since a large part of your kit is spell-based, not missing is essential.<h2 class="heading-size-2 heading-bar" id="gemming">
<span data-markup-content-target="1">
<span class="iconmedium" data-color="c6" data-contiguous="true" data-env="mists" data-game="wow" data-kind="round" data-tree="mists">
<ins class="lazy-load-background" style='background-image: url("https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/medium/inv_misc_gem_x4_rare_uncut_purple.jpg");'></ins>
<del></del></span> Gemming for Unholy Death Knights</span></h2>Death Knight has always been a class that feels better with gear. The more you Crit and the more Haste you have, the more fun your character is to play. Gems are also a great place to grab any Hit and Expertise that we may be missing. This can be especially useful when aiming for Socket Bonuses, as <a class="q3 icontiny" data-contiguous="true" data-entity="item" data-entity-has-icon="true" data-game="wow" data-type="item" href="https://www.wowhead.com/mop-classic/item=76641/piercing-wild-jade">
<img alt="Piercing Wild Jade" loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/inv_misc_gem_x4_rare_cut_green.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Piercing Wild Jade</span></a> is typically the only good way to utilize a Blue Socket if we want the bonus.<br/>
<br/>The Unholy Death Knight has a point around item level 540~ where it becomes more valuable to gem towards crit rather than strength. This means the gemming priority might be slightly different for you, depending on gear level. <br/>
